# Model card: platedet v$version

A small detector that finds **licence plates** and **vehicles** in a photo. It returns a box,
a class and a confidence score for each one.

## Intended use

- **Privacy:** find plates so an app can blur or mask them before a photo is stored or shared.
- **Photo checks:** confirm that a photo contains a vehicle.
- **One photo at a time,** on phones (TFLite FP16) or servers and desktops (ONNX). The
  model was not tuned for live video.
- **Worldwide plates.** The training data is mostly US, UK, continental European and
  Chinese (CCPD) plates, so it is less tested elsewhere (see Limits).

**Not intended for:** reading plate text (it has no OCR), identifying or tracking people or
vehicles, or any use where a missed plate creates a legal or safety risk without human review.

## Files

| File | Use |
|---|---|
| `detector.onnx` | FP32, $onnx_mb MB, input NCHW `(1, 3, 640, 640)` |
| `detector_fp16.tflite` | FP16 weights, $fp16_mb MB, input NHWC `(1, 640, 640, 3)`, float32 in and out |

There is no INT8 file in this release: it failed the accuracy check against ONNX.
Integration steps are in `README.md`; the exact contract is in `release.json`.

## How to run it (summary)

- Letterbox to 640 × 640, BGR, raw 0–255 values. Decode the `(1, 8400, 7)` output, apply
  class-aware NMS (IoU 0.45) and map the boxes back to the photo.
- **Recommended thresholds:** plate $plate_threshold, vehicle $vehicle_threshold. They were
  chosen on the validation set; a low plate threshold favours not missing plates.
- **Recommended mode: 2 × 2 tiles.** Run the whole photo plus 4 overlapping tiles and merge.
  This finds far more small, distant plates. It takes about 5 model passes per photo.

## Accuracy

Measured with ONNX FP32 and 2 × 2 tiles. The phone file gives the same boxes (see Parity).

- **Test set:** $test_images photos and $test_plates readable plates from the same sources as
  training. It was never used for training or tuning.
- **Holdout:** $holdout_images photos and $holdout_plates plates from the Open Images test split,
  each reviewed by hand. It is closer to real-world photos.
- **Labels:** both sets were audited (wrong labels fixed or ignored). Plates under 12 px
  wide are ignored because they can't be read.

### At the recommended thresholds

| | Test | Holdout |
|---|---|---|
| Plate recall | $test_plate_recall | $holdout_plate_recall |
| Small plates (12–32 px wide) recall | $test_small | $holdout_small |
| False plate boxes per photo | $test_plate_fp | $holdout_plate_fp |
| Vehicle recall | $test_vehicle_recall | $holdout_vehicle_recall |
| False vehicle boxes per photo | $test_vehicle_fp | $holdout_vehicle_fp |

The holdout has plate labels only, so it has no vehicle numbers.

### Project targets

These are measured at fixed thresholds (plate 0.2, vehicle 0.3), so they stay comparable
between releases.

| Metric | Value | Target | Met |
|---|---|---|---|
$spec_rows

v$version misses some of the targets. The project owner reviewed and accepted the gap for
this release.

- At the targets' plate threshold of 0.2, plate recall is just under 0.95 on the test set
  and 0.90 on the harder holdout. At the recommended 0.10 it is higher (see above).
- Vehicle recall is low partly because the vehicle labels include close-ups and interior
  shots that a detector struggles to box. It is fine for "is there a vehicle in this photo".

## Parity: phone vs server model

The FP16 file was checked against ONNX FP32 on $parity_images test and holdout photos.

- **Single pass:** all $single_boxes boxes match (box overlap ≥ 0.9, score within 0.05).
- **2 × 2 tiles:** $tiles_off of $tiles_boxes boxes differ slightly, and $tiles_missing are
  missing. The differences are near-ties where two passes find the same object.

**Speed** on an Apple M2 Pro CPU (median):

| | One pass | Photo with 2 × 2 tiles |
|---|---|---|
| ONNX | $onnx_pass_ms ms | $onnx_ms ms |
| TFLite FP16, 4 threads | $fp16_pass_ms ms | $fp16_ms ms |

Phones vary; GPU or NNAPI/Core ML delegates can be faster.

## Limits

- **Dark photos at steep angles:** plates in dim light, seen at a sharp angle or very
  close up and cut off at the photo edge, can be missed entirely.
- **Small plates:** plates under about 12 px wide are not expected to be found. Of plates
  12–32 px wide, $test_small_missed% are missed on the test set and $holdout_small_missed% on the
  harder holdout.
- **Regions:** few training plates from Africa, South America, the Middle East, South and
  South-East Asia, Japan or Korea. Test on your own photos before relying on it there.
- **Look-alikes:** signs, stickers and dealer inserts are sometimes boxed as plates.
  Dealer inserts and blanked plates count as plates by design.
- **Vehicles:** close-ups, partial vehicles and interiors are often missed or boxed loosely.
- **Plates away from vehicles** (on a wall, held in a hand) are likely to be boxed as plates
  too; the model doesn't check that a plate is on a vehicle.

## Training data

- **9,503 training photos** with 10,427 plate boxes and 16,930 vehicle boxes:
  - Open Images V6 (CC BY 2.0): 6,113 photos;
  - CCPD 2020 (MIT): 3,150 Chinese green-plate photos;
  - Kaggle "Car License Plate Detection" (CC0): 240 photos.
- About 4,500 more vehicle boxes were machine-generated, by a larger YOLOX model, on the
  plate-only photos.
- **Excluded:** sources with non-commercial, GPL or AGPL licences, or unknown licences.
- **Credits:** every training image is credited in `ATTRIBUTION.md`.

## Model

- **YOLOX-Tiny:** about 5 M parameters, 640 × 640 input, anchor-free, strides 8/16/32.
- **Starting point:** the Megvii COCO checkpoint (Apache-2.0).
- **Training:** 100 epochs; the checkpoint was picked on the validation set.
- **Provenance:** checkpoint SHA-256 `$checkpoint_sha`; release commit `$commit`.
  All file hashes are in `release.json` and `SHA256SUMS`.

